{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":270850,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"913b9f64-9880-4a54-bfe1-41e10767336a","total_cost_usd":1.4148674999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":77222,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":23160,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":77222,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":23160,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":77222,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":77222},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":55069,"outputTokens":17,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.055154,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":23160,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":77222,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.3597134999999998,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"spec-auditor\",\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"summary\": \"The flight, ablation and economy numbers hold up — the skim multiplier is now the flown one and the parachute claim is finally independent — but the module cannot come home on two passes from where it hangs (243.2 against a 223 ceiling, and no capacity closes that gap), and the shipping slice is too light to load either the ship or the canopy: a full hold is 1.23x, not 2x, and lands at 3.22 m/s.\",\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"single_band_all_pieces_inside\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"There is exactly one band and every catalog piece sits inside it; params use only the sample keys bottom, middle, top.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"bands[0] is 50,000-280,000 m and is the only band. All 30 catalog altitudes fall inside it: lowest torn_foil_blanket 52,500, highest armstrongs_module 277,000. Sample keys appearing in params: ablation.heat_index {bottom, middle, top}, ablation.cost_curve {bottom, middle, top}, ablation.optimal_skims {bottom, middle, top} — no fourth key anywhere.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_summary_matches_catalog\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band_summary entry agrees with the debris array it summarises: piece count, mean mass, fragile fraction.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Third boundaries at 126,667 and 203,333 m. Bottom: 11 pieces, masses 15+24+21+30+36+82+34+46+50+54+48 = 440, mean 40.0 (claimed 40); fragile spawn weight 3+2+2+2 = 9 of 90 = 0.100 (claimed 0.100). Middle: 10 pieces, sum 805, mean 80.5 (claimed 80.5); fragile 3+4+3+2 = 12 of 69 = 0.17391 (claimed 0.1739). Top: 9 pieces, sum 4,449, mean 494.33 (claimed 494.33); fragile 3+2+3+2 = 10 of 40 = 0.250 (claimed 0.25). Reported altitudes 88,333 / 165,000 / 241,667 are the exact third midpoints.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"mass_rises_with_altitude\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"The catalog's mean piece mass rises with altitude, third over third.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Mean mass 40.0 -> 80.5 -> 494.33 kg, strictly rising. Excluding armstrongs_module the top third still reads (4,449-3,600)/8 = 106.1 kg, so the rise is 40.0 -> 80.5 -> 106.1 on ordinary pieces alone: 2.01x then 1.32x, no flat or inverted step.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_share_rises_with_altitude\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile spawn share rises with altitude, roughly 1 in 10 at the bottom to 1 in 4 at the top.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted fragile share 9/90 = 0.100, 12/69 = 0.174, 10/40 = 0.250 — exactly 1-in-10 to 1-in-4, monotonic. Counted rather than weighted it would read 4/11, 4/10, 4/9 = 0.36/0.40/0.44, nearly flat, so the gradient is carried by the weights as intended.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"full_hold_doubles_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"A full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Recomputed independently from the catalog: spawn-weighted over the shipping slice, hand-tetherable only, at compactor_tier 1, mass*weight sums to 8,126 kg over 267 weighted slots = 30.43 kg/slot. 6 base slots x 30.43 = 182.6 kg against dry_mass_kg 800 = 1.23x. Read the other way the GDD 4.3 allows — one slot per piece — the spawn-weighted mean piece is 8,126/155 = 52.4 kg, so 6 slots = 314.5 kg and 1.39x. Both are outside the 1.5-2.5x that 'roughly doubles' permits, low by a factor of 1.63 on the first reading. Dry mass cannot absorb it: 183 kg dry gives a staged ballistic coefficient of 183/(1.4*3.5) = 37, under the ~50 floor at which the atmosphere stops the ship instead of braking it, which deletes the skim mechanic the endgame rests on. The catalog's shipping-slice masses are the only lever left.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise mass_kg across the hand-tetherable shipping slice toward roughly 133 kg per slot (about 4.4x present), or state plainly that the doubling sentence cannot hold with this catalog.\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_cost_curve_optimum_in_range\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Each cost_curve minimum equals its optimal_skims entry, and the curve is what the stated ablation model actually produces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Model as stated in balance_notes and as the charter defines it: cumulative tolls 5.350 / 11.3955 / 18.2269 / 25.9464 (5.35 * 1.13^i); each skim adds 7e-6 * 43.9^3 = 0.5922; entry term 7e-6 * (heat_index * mult[k])^3. Recomputed all twelve points. Bottom: 5.350+7.000 = 12.350; 11.3955+0.4650+0.5922 = 12.453; 18.2269+0.4650+1.1845 = 19.876; 25.9464+0.4650+1.7767 = 28.188 — array [12.35, 12.45, 19.88, 28.19], max error 0.03%. Middle: 19.319 / 12.916 / 20.339 / 28.651 against [19.32, 12.92, 20.34, 28.65]. Top: 35.056 / 13.961 / 21.385 / 29.697 against [35.06, 13.96, 21.38, 29.70]. Argmins are k=0, k=1, k=1, matching optimal_skims {bottom 0, middle 1, top 1} exactly. The bottom's 12.350 vs 12.453 is a 0.8% margin but the argmin is still 0.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"heavy_descent_requires_multi_pass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"The endgame haul cannot stay under the bar on a single pass at any entry depth, can on two, while an empty ship and a full hold still can on one from anywhere in the band.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"commit_floor_m is 8,000 m — 35,000 m below atmosphere_top_m 43,000 — so the floor genuinely constrains the entry and the check is live. Judged on committed_descents only. Part 1 holds: the endgame haul's plunge peaks 223.7 / 268.5 / 335.5 at bottom / middle / top, all above heat_capacity 219 and above every purchasable tier (221, 223). Part 3 holds: empty 93.8 / 116.0 / 147.8 and full hold 104.3 / 128.6 / 163.4, all under 219 from anywhere, so ordinary cargo is never forced to aerobrake. Part 2 fails: one skim (two passes) brings the module to 208.2 from the bottom but only 224.4 from the middle and 243.2 from the top — both above the 223 ceiling — and the module hangs at 277,000 m, above the top sample. It needs two skims, three heat cycles, to reach 213.9 / 217.8. No heat_capacity fixes this: satisfying part 1 requires C < 223.7 (the bottom plunge) and satisfying part 2 requires C > 243.2 (the top one-skim), and 243.2 > 223.7, so the two bounds are contradictory. The rule as written cannot be satisfied by any capacity at this dry mass and drag configuration.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"No heat_capacity value satisfies both bounds — reshape the module's peaks (dry_mass_kg or the shield drag configuration) or record that the finale is a three-cycle descent and the two-pass form of the rule is withdrawn.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_multiplier_matches_flight\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed skim_heat_multiplier matches what the simulator flew at the top sample, within 0.10 at every index.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"params.ablation.skim_heat_multiplier [1, 0.405, 0.405, 0.405] against skims.top.skim_heat_multiplier_measured [1, 0.405, 0.405, 0.405]: delta 0.000 at indices 0, 1, 2 and 3. The array is entry-for-entry the flown top-sample study, so every cost in cost_curve is priced off measured physics. Noted for the record: the bottom flew 0.702 and the middle 0.526, so the adopted top array overstates the benefit of skimming lower in the band by up to 1.73x, which is why the bottom's cost_curve optimum is a 0.8% tie.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_pricing_and_thermal_fatigue\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"skim_heat_multiplier starts at 1.0, is non-increasing and flattens by its last two entries; cycle_toll_growth exceeds 1.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"[1, 0.405, 0.405, 0.405]: starts at 1.0, non-increasing at every step (1 -> 0.405 -> 0.405 -> 0.405), and the last two entries are identical, so the benefit saturates rather than claiming a physics that was measured not to exist. cycle_toll_growth 1.13 > 1 with cycle_toll_base_pct 5.35, so 25 cycles cost 5.35 * (1.13^25 - 1)/0.13 = 832.5% of plate plus 24 skim terms at 0.592 and one entry term = 848.7%; a flat toll would cost 25 * 5.35 = 133.8% and would not bound anyone.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_speed_under_soft_threshold\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Full-hold parachute descent speed is under the 5 m/s soft-landing line but above about 3.5 m/s, so the Parachute upgrade is a real purchase.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"v = sqrt(2mg / (rho0 * Cd * A)) = sqrt(2 * 982.6 * 9.0 / (1.5 * 1.8 * 630)) = sqrt(17,686.8/1,701) = 3.22 m/s, and the integrator lands it at 3.22 at all three sample altitudes. That is 0.28 m/s under the 3.5 floor — soft with no purchase and no margin to sell. The cause is the mass under the canopy, not the canopy: at 630 m2 and Cd 1.8, reaching 3.5 m/s needs 3.5^2 * 94.5 = 1,157.6 kg loaded, i.e. 357.6 kg of cargo across 6 slots, 59.6 kg/slot against the catalog's 30.43. The canopy itself cannot be the lever — 630 m2 and Cd 1.8 are GDD 2.3 constants and shrinking them is the tautology that rule exists to forbid.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise the shipping slice's per-slot mass so a full hold actually loads the canopy; leave parachute_area_m2 at the GDD's 630.\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_speed_matches_flight\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed descent speed is what the stated canopy actually flies, measured independently.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"params state parachute_area_m2 630 and parachute_drag_coefficient 1.8, and the measured parachute block reports independent true — so the area was not solved backwards out of the claim. claimed_full_hold_ms 3.23 against measured_full_hold_ms 3.22, delta_ms 0.01 = 0.31% of the claim, well inside the 5% bound, with spread_across_bands_ms 0.00. This is the first run in which the rule has actually been tested rather than passing against itself.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"tow_fee_clamps_at_half\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"The tow fee is zero inside the free radius, linear beyond it, clamps at exactly 50% of haul value, and can never be negative.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"tow_fee.free_radius_m 1200 (GDD 2.5's ~1,200 m), curve 'linear', max_fee_fraction exactly 0.5. Fee(d) = 0 for d <= 1200 and min(k*(d-1200), 0.5*haul) beyond, so it is zero inside the radius, non-negative everywhere (d-1200 >= 0 on the active branch), and bounded above at half the haul. On the lazy six-panel run grossing 268.2 the worst possible fee is 134.1, leaving 134.1 against a 110 launch — the clamp does exactly the job GDD 2.5 gives it.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"lazy_run_breaks_even\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Launch cost is at or below the value of three cheap pieces near the floor of the band.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Multiplier m(h) = 1 + 4.5*(h-50,000)/230,000. The three cheapest non-fragile smalls off the floor: crumpled_panel_stack 56,000 m, m=1.1174, 40*1.1174 = 44.70; torn_foil_blanket 52,500 m, m=1.0489, 41.96 (fragile, so 125.9 intact); spent_kick_motor_casing 73,500 m, m=1.4598, 58.39. Three panel stacks alone are 134.1 and the cheapest realistic trio is 145.1, both above launch_cost 110. Two panel stacks are 89.4, so the launch prices at 2.5 cheap pieces — GDD 2.5's 'value of 2-3 cheap pieces' read straight off this catalog. A six-slot panel run grosses 268.2 and nets 133.5 after the stage and 12.35% of plate at 2/pct.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"towing_never_shears\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Steady-state tow tension at the heaviest legal two-piece load stays below magnet_hold_force_n with margin.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Heaviest legal two-piece hand-tetherable load in the shipping slice is dead_thruster_quad 70 kg plus shattered_star_tracker 72 kg = 142 kg. a = 520/(110+142) = 2.063 m/s2, so per-cable tension is 72*2.063 = 148.6 N and 70*2.063 = 144.4 N against magnet_hold_force_n 900 — a 6.06x margin. The bound is structural: tension = 520*m/(110+M) < 520 for any load whatever, so steady tow can never shear at 900 N. Worst case in the game, Armstrong's module: a = 520/3,710 = 0.1402 m/s2, tension 3,600*0.1402 = 504.6 N, still 1.78x under the hold force.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"staging_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No parameter reintroduces thrust after staging.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The only thrust-bearing params are flight.thrust_n 52,000 and flight.rcs_thrust_n 1,800, both drawing on flight.fuel_capacity_kg 1,600 via fuel_burn_kgs 18.6 and rcs_fuel_burn_kgs 0.6. Staging jettisons thruster and tank, and there is no separate post-stage propellant reserve, no restart or relight field, and no upgrade that adds one — the twelve upgrade effects are fuel_capacity_kg, thrust_n, base_slots, heat_capacity, parachute_area_m2 and magnet_range_screens. eva.jetpack_thrust_n 520 belongs to the astronaut, not the staged hull.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_never_crushable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile pieces are uncrushable at every compactor tier present, and the fragile premium makes one intact fragile piece worth several solid pieces of the same class nearby.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cargo.compactor_tier is 1, the only tier present. All twelve fragile catalog entries carry uncrushable true (foil blanket, solar array, antenna mesh, radiator fin, optics barrel, star tracker, ablative nose cone, avionics rack, deep space dish, ion grid, cryo dewar, command module hatch), and all three size_classes carry fragile_uncrushable true, so no tier can buy the flag off. Premium check at matched class and altitude: cracked_deep_space_dish (medium, fragile, 217,500 m) prices at 90 * 4.2772 * 3 = 1,154.8 against torn_reactor_shield_plate (medium, solid, 209,000 m) at 90 * 4.1109 = 370.0 — 3.12 solid pieces per fragile one.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"oversized_rejects_hand_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oversized junk cannot be taken by the hand magnet.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"size_classes.oversized.hand_tetherable is false, against true for small and medium. The three oversized pieces — collapsed_dish_frame 86,500 m, buckled_pressure_bulkhead 160,000 m, severed_boom_mast 234,000 m — are therefore unreachable by the tether, and the first sits inside the shipping slice at 86,500 m so the player meets the refusal early and reads it as a rule about the tool.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"twelve_upgrade_purchases\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Twelve purchases: six parts at two tiers each, tier 2 costing more than tier 1, each tier improving the stat it names.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Exactly 12 entries over 6 parts. Costs rise tier 1 -> tier 2: fuel_tank 400->1,100, thruster 450->1,200, storage 600->1,500, heat_shield 700->1,600, parachute 350->1,300, hand_magnet 250->700. Each tier improves its stat over the base and over tier 1: fuel_capacity_kg 1,600->2,400->3,000; thrust_n 52,000->64,000->75,000; base_slots 6->8->10; heat_capacity 219->221->223; parachute_area_m2 630->900->1,250; magnet_range_screens 0.125->0.1875->0.25. Shop total 10,150.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"launch_survives_itself\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"The climb's peak heat stays under reentry.heat_capacity.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"climb_peak_heat is 50.0 (bottom), 47.5 (middle), 46.7 (top); the hottest is 50.0 against heat_capacity 219 — 22.8% of the bar, leaving 77% unused, and climb_survivable is true on all three ascents. The middle sample's 47.5 reproduces GDD 2.2's stated ascent peak exactly. The player meets the heat gauge on the first launch somewhere it cannot kill them, which is what the rule is for.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"shipping_slice_reachable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"The bottom two thirds of the envelope are reachable by the base ship, and the catalog populates both thirds with fragility and at least two size classes.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Judged at the band floor on an arc: the bottom sample (65,000 m) reports arc_reached true, apex 73,749 m, a 125.9 s EVA window and 29.4% of tank left — GDD 4.5's open question about 97 seconds is answered generously. It also circularises there with 6.9%. The middle sample (115,000 m) is reached on an arc at apex 131,246 m with a 202.1 s window and 23.8% margin, so the whole slice up to 203,333 m is arc-reachable; the top is arc-only ('out of fuel before circularising'), which is the progression beat rather than a failure. Catalog population: bottom third carries small, medium and oversized classes and 4 fragile pieces (share 0.100); middle third carries small, medium and oversized and 4 fragile pieces (share 0.174).\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"no_target_moved_to_pass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No rule passes because a number the design document fixes in prose was moved to meet it.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Every GDD-stated quantity is at its stated value: cargo.base_slots 6 and eva.base_tether_slots 2 (2.4, 2.5), reentry.commit_floor_m 8,000 (2.2), landing.parachute_area_m2 630 with drag coefficient 1.8 (2.3), landing.soft_landing_ms 5 and no_gear_multiplier 2 (2.3), reentry.unstaged_heat_multiplier 3, heat_dissipation_s 5, cargo_damage_interval_s 3 (2.2), tow free radius 1,200 m and max fee 0.5 (2.5), value gradient 1.0 at floor to 5.5 at ceiling across 50,000-280,000 m (2.6), armstrongs_module 3,600 kg (2.6). Two rules are reported broken rather than bought: the full-hold doubling is left at 1.23x instead of being satisfied by dropping dry_mass_kg to 183, and the canopy is left at 630 m2 instead of the 72 m2 that would hold 4.4 m/s fixed. That is the correct behaviour under this rule — the failures above are real findings, not tuning artefacts.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"observations\": [\n    \"The heat-shield track buys almost nothing. 219 -> 221 -> 223 is +0.9% per tier for 700 and 1,600 credits — 4 bar units total across the shop's most expensive progression line. GDD 2.5 also makes the shield the only mass-reducing purchase, the counterweight to storage-driven landing difficulty, and dry_mass_kg stays 800 at every tier. A player who buys both tiers will not feel either.\",\n    \"The module's entire survivable window is (217.8, 223.7) — 2.7% wide, with the whole capacity ladder squeezed inside it. The Balancer records the window closing to 0.8% at dry mass 900 and going negative at 1,000. The endgame therefore rests on a ~100 kg margin in a parameter no rule guards; anything that touches dry mass, shield drag or reference area will delete the win condition silently.\",\n    \"Storage is only half double-edged. Tier 2 costs 1,500, raises the profit ceiling 67%, and moves full-hold touchdown from 3.22 to 3.42 m/s — both soft on the base canopy with no compensating purchase. GDD 2.5's 'raises the difficulty of the flight home' is not in these numbers, and the spread across the whole greed decision (empty 2.91 to full 3.22 m/s) is 0.31 m/s.\",\n    \"The base ship can circularise at the middle sample with 2.9% of tank — 46.4 kg — which the sweep scores as reached but which leaves nothing to deorbit on. That is a route the player can legally take one band above where the design says orbit should already be unaffordable, and it ends in GDD 2.7's stranded state.\",\n    \"Value carries no mass term. cracked_deep_space_dish (78 kg, medium, fragile, 217,500 m) prices at 1,154.8 while cracked_command_module_hatch (112 kg, small, fragile, 268,000 m) prices at 40 * 4.7391 * 3 = 568.7 — the heavier, higher piece is worth half the lighter one. GDD 2.6's 'value scales with mass on top of the altitude gradient' is approximated by the catalog's masses rising with altitude, but the coupling itself is absent from economy.\",\n    \"The staged-versus-unstaged braking choice is unverified where it matters. unstaged_braking carries rows for bottom (71) and middle (78) and none for the top, and the exploration grid satisfies unstaged survivability in 8.0% of configurations. The choice GDD 2.2 describes may simply not exist at the altitude the finale is flown from.\",\n    \"One arithmetic slip in balance_notes worth correcting even though nothing fails on it: the module's steady tow tension is quoted as 388.2 N, but the note's own formula gives 520 * 3,600 / (110 + 3,600) = 504.6 N. Still 1.78x under the 900 N hold force, so the rule holds either way.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":162185,"ttft_stream_ms":1819,"time_to_request_ms":198,"type":"result","duration_ms":267325,"uuid":"77b56808-e8f2-4000-a787-cc48397ee710"}
